{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"what-survives","type":"text","text":"# What Survives Schmid\n\n**These data sources are independently dated and survive\neven if Schmid's late-dating thesis is correct:**\n\n• Ugaritic texts (14th-13th c.) — ANE comparative baseline\n• Soleb Shasu (14th c.) — earliest Yahweh reference\n• Merneptah Stele (13th c.) — earliest \"Israel\" reference\n• Kuntillet Ajrud & Khirbet el-Qom (9th-8th c.) — inscriptions\n• Hebrew onomastics (Tigay) — names on seals, bullae, ostraca\n• Elephantine Papyri (5th c.) — Persian-period Jewish practice\n• Archaeological record — cult sites, figurines, standing stones\n\n**What these DO show:** polytheistic Yahwism, Asherah devotion,\nparallel elite/folk religions, and Yahweh's southern origins —\nall independently of the biblical text's dating.\n\n→ [[notes/synthesis/capstone-what-does-this-mean]]","x":-485,"y":1280,"width":1050,"height":420,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"kuntillet-ajrud","type":"text","text":"## c. 800 BCE: Kuntillet Ajrud\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nIndependently dated inscriptions.\n\"Yahweh of Samaria\" / \"Yahweh of Teman.\"\n\"Yahweh and his Asherah\" blessing\nformula.","x":80,"y":105,"width":280,"height":230,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"khirbet-el-qom","type":"text","text":"## c. 750-700 BCE: Khirbet el-Qom\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nIndependently dated tomb inscription.\n\"Blessed by Yahweh and his Asherah.\"\nParallel to Kuntillet Ajrud formula.","x":640,"y":-95,"width":280,"height":210,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"exile","type":"text","text":"## 586-539 BCE: Babylonian Exile\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nIndependently attested historical event.\nNebuchadnezzar's destruction of Jerusalem.\nBabylonian Chronicle corroboration.","x":1505,"y":320,"width":280,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"second-isaiah","type":"text","text":"## c. 540 BCE: Second Isaiah\n**Schmid Risk: LOW-MEDIUM**\n\nIsa 40-55; first unambiguous monotheism.\n\"I am Yahweh, there is no other\" (Isa 45:5).\nExilic setting widely accepted.","x":1920,"y":-80,"width":280,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"elephantine","type":"text","text":"## 5th c. BCE: Elephantine Papyri\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nIndependently dated Jewish community\narchive. Anat-Yahu worshipped alongside\nYahweh. Polytheistic Yahwism in Persian\nperiod.","x":2320,"y":320,"width":280,"height":230,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"persian-period","type":"text","text":"## 5th-4th c. BCE: Persian Period\n**THIS IS THE CHALLENGE**\n\nSchmid: most biblical texts composed NOW.\nNot earlier traditions preserved — but\nretrojections. If correct: Torah as Persian\nimperial authorization document.","x":2680,"y":-310,"width":300,"height":230,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"merneptah","type":"text","text":"## c. 1208 BCE: Merneptah Stele\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nEgyptian victory stele; independently dated.\n\"Israel is laid waste; his seed is not.\"\nEarliest extra-biblical mention of Israel.","x":-1514,"y":-280,"width":280,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ugaritic","type":"text","text":"## 14th-13th c. BCE: Ugaritic Texts\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nIndependently dated Canaanite library.\nDivine council, Baal Cycle, El as high god.\nCritical ANE comparative material.\n\n[[sources/primary-sources/ugaritic-baal-cycle]]","x":-1794,"y":120,"width":280,"height":230,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"soleb-shasu","type":"text","text":"## c. 1370 BCE: Soleb Shasu\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nIndependently dated Egyptian inscription.\nFirst known reference to \"Yahweh.\"\nShasu of Yhw in region of Edom/Seir.\n\n[[sources/primary-sources/soleb-shasu-inscription]]","x":-2320,"y":-215,"width":280,"height":230,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"schmid-zone","type":"text","text":"# Schmid Zone Overlay\n\nIf Schmid is correct, **all pre-exilic textual data points**\nare Persian-period retrojections. The biblical text is not a\nwindow into Iron Age Israelite religion — it is a Persian-period\nconstruction projecting its theology backward.\n\n**Implication:** the entire Documentary Hypothesis chronology\ncollapses. J, E, D, and P are not successive pre-exilic sources\nbut post-exilic literary strata.\n\n→ [[notes/synthesis/phase3-counter-position-stress-tests]]","x":565,"y":-1520,"width":950,"height":300,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"hebrew-inscriptions","type":"text","text":"## 8th-6th c. BCE: Hebrew Inscriptions\n**Schmid Risk: LOW**\n\nTigay onomastic study: seals, bullae,\nostraca. ~89% Yahwistic theophoric names.\nIndependent of biblical text.","x":980,"y":350,"width":280,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"earliest-poetry","type":"text","text":"## c. 1200-1000 BCE: Earliest Biblical Poetry?\n**Schmid Risk: HIGH**\n\nJudg 5, Exod 15, Deut 33, Hab 3.\nConventional: Iron I archaic Hebrew.\nSchmid: Persian-period compositions.","x":-880,"y":25,"width":300,"height":210,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"je-sources","type":"text","text":"## c. 1000-900 BCE: J and E Sources?\n**Schmid Risk: HIGH**\n\nDocumentary Hypothesis anchors.\nConventional: early monarchy context.\nSchmid: exilic/post-exilic narrative\nconstruction.","x":-240,"y":-280,"width":280,"height":220,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"dark-age","type":"text","text":"# The 500-Year Textual Dark Age\n\n**c. 1200–700 BCE (Ugarit → Persian period)**\n\nNo contemporary textual evidence for Israelite religion\nduring this span — unless the Bible is pre-exilic.\n\nIf Schmid is right, this gap extends to c. 500 BCE:\na **700-year** dark age for textual sources.","x":-1585,"y":-1495,"width":1100,"height":250,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"josiah-reform","type":"text","text":"## c. 622 BCE: Josiah's Reform\n**Schmid Risk: MEDIUM**\n\n2 Kings 22-23; Arad temple closure\nconfirmed archaeologically.\nConventional: historical late-7th-c. event.\nSchmid: narrative shaped in Persian period.","x":1040,"y":-510,"width":290,"height":230,"color":"3"}
	],
